## 1. Introduction
The use of renewable agricultural-based fuels in diesel cycle engines has been an attractive option for many years from environmental and energy safety perspective [1]. Biodiesel is fatty acid alkyl esters (FAAEs) derived from renewable sources of long-chain triglycerides, such as vegetable oils and animal fats [2,3]. Life-cycle analyses (LCA) shows that biodiesel has a positive energy balance, indicating the overall environmental benefits of biodiesel production and use [4,5]. In addition, biodiesel has better biodegradability, higher combustion efficiency, and lower sulfur and aromatic content than the petroleum-derived diesel [6,7]. Biodiesel has similar fuel characteristics to diesel and can be completely miscible in any proportion [8]. B20 and B5 (Indicates the proportion of biodiesel) are the most common blends and could be used in engines without any modifications [9].   
Biodiesel is generally obtained by transesterification of triglycerides with alcohols, including methanol, ethanol, propanol, etc. Among these alcohols, methanol is the most commonly used because of its low cost [10]. Although many feedstock‟s have been successfully utilized for the production of biodiesel, it is currently being produced from edible vegetable oils and discarded oils due to the economic feasibility [11]. The type of vegetable oil used in biodiesel production is determined by the region, such as soybean oil in the United States and Brazil, palm oil in Southeast Asia, and coconut oil in Thailand [12]. Besides, waste oil, such as waste cooking oil, can also be used as a cost-effective alternative to pure vegetable oil for biodiesel production [13].
Since catalysts can significantly accelerate the reaction rate, the current focus of biodiesel research is directed to evaluate the performance of different catalysts and their impact on biodiesel production processes [14]. The most commonly used catalysts in biodiesel production are basic catalysts such as KOH, NaOH, which have robust catalytic activity, short reaction times and high productivity [15]. However, alkali processes exhibit some disadvantages such as high energy requirements, difficult recovery of the catalyst and glycerol, and environment pollution [16].
In recent years, enzymatic transesterification using lipase has attracted the interest of researchers for biodiesel production. Fig. 1 represents the lipase-catalyzed transesterification reaction. The ability of the lipase (EC 3.1.1.3, triacylglycerol hydrolases) to maintain remarkable catalytic activity in non-aqueous media render it possible use in biodiesel production. Compared with the chemical catalysis, the biodiesel production by lipase displays numerous advantages, such as easy product separation, minimal wastewater treatment requirements, easy glycerol recovery, and the absence of side reactions [17, 18]. The high temperature environment required for alkaline catalysis can promote the hydrolysis of triglycerides, producing a large amount of free fatty acids (FFAs) in waste cooking oil, which causes some problems in alkaline catalytic production of biodiesel [19]. On contrary, lipase has the ability to convert both FFAs and triglycerides into FAAEs, so it can be used to produce biodiesel from feedstocks with high FFAs content in mild reaction conditions [20, 21]. Low yields and high costs are the major drawbacks of  biodiesel production using the free lipase, which limits the large-scale application of enzymatic methods [18].
Immobilization technique has been introduced to improve lipase stability and to reduce production costs [22]. It has been found that the immobilized enzymes can be regenerated and reused several times, which can substantially reduce the cost [23]. Furthermore, the catalytic stability of enzymes can be improved after immobilization [24]. Biodiesel is typically produced in low water content media, where free lipase is not properly dispersed. The immobilization prevents agglomeration of the enzyme under low water conditions, increases dispersibility, and thereby increases the catalytic activity. Besides, immobilized enzyme technology also makes it possible to use packed bed reactors in enzymatic biodiesel production, which is more cost effective [25].
Conventional immobilized enzyme materials are typically micron sized. In recent years, nanostructured materials have received extensive attention in biosensors, bio-catalysis, pollution mitigation, drug delivery, etc. [26-28]. Compared with the traditional materials, nanomaterials have been emerged as novel and promising supports for enzymes immobilization due to large specific surface area, low mass transfer resistance, superior loading capacity and many other advantages [29-31]. This review focuses on biodiesel production from the waste by using immobilized lipase in/on nanomaterials through various techniques and the parameters that affect their functionality. It explicitly unveils some important aspects that are not well covered previously. The information in this review will be useful for improving biodiesel production by using immobilized lipase in/on nanomaterials and interesting for readers with the broad spectrum biotechnological backgrounds.
## 2. Strategies for lipase immobilization on/in nanomaterials
In the past ten years, different nanomaterials such as nanoparticles, nanotubes and nanofibrous membranes have been used for the immobilization of lipases. Lipases immobilization in/on nanomaterials for biodiesel production are summarized in Table 1.
## 2.1 Lipase immobilization on magnetic nanoparticles
Magnetic nanoparticles are increasingly used in biological sciences and medicine because of their easier separation ability from the reaction medium by applying an external magnetic field [32]. In lipase immobilization, the most commonly used magnetic support is magnetite $\mathrm{(Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4})$ . Some other ferrites like maghemite $\left(\gamma{-}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}\right)$ were also studied as carriers [33]. Since bare $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ is susceptible to oxidation and acidic environments, whereas pure magnetite cannot interact effectively with the biological molecules (proteins or enzymes). Therefore, some surface functional modification methods have been developed to overcome the above-mentioned drawbacks and to improve the enzyme-carrying capacity of magnetic nanoparticles. Amino silanes such as (3-aminopropyl)triethoxysilane (APTES) are the most commonly used amino-functional reagents for magnetic nanoparticle modification [2]. These silanes can be conveniently polymerized on the surface of magnetic nanoparticles to form a core-shell structure with an amino group on the surface [34]. The lipase can be covalently coupled to the amino group of the carrier by a multi-functional reagent such as glutaraldehyde (GA) to constitute a magnetic biocatalyst [35]. Using this immobilization strategy, Xie et al. [36] utilized GA to immobilize Thermomyces lanuginosa lipases (TLL) on the APTES coated magnetic nanoparticles. Results revealed that the immobilized lipase exhibited $70\%$ , and $84\%$ of the activity recovery and immobilization efficiency, respectively, and applied for biodiesel production from soybean oil by the three-step addition of methanol. The conversion rate of methanol reached $90\%$ . Similarly, Miao et al. [37] immobilized Candida antarctica lipase B (CALB) on APTES-modified magnetic nanoparticles to biosynthesize biodiesel by rapeseed oil. The maximum conversion rate to methyl ester was $89.4\%$ . Furthermore, the immobilized biocatalyst retained $70\%$ of its initial activity after reusing for 5 cycles. Jambulingam et al. [38] coupled lipase to the APTES-coated magnetic nanoparticles through 1-Ethyl-3-(Dimethylamino Propyl)Carbodiimide (EDC) and N-Hydroxysuccinimide (NHS), and the resulting immobilized lipase was successfully used for the conversion of Aspergillus lipid to biodiesel.
Compared to the direct modification of bare $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , the silicon layer can provide abundant silanol groups and can react with organosilicon compounds more easily [39]. Thangaraj et al. [40] encapsulated the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ particles with mesoporous silicon and subjected to modification with APTES or (3-mercaptopropyl) trimethoxysilane (MPTMS). The modified support was then used to bind the lipase by using glutaraldehyde as a cross-linking agent. During the conversion of soybean oil to biodiesel, the authors found that the catalytic activity of immobilized lipase on APTES-modified $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ particles was comparatively better than that of MPTMS. This method has a high yield of biodiesel $(>90\%)$ .
Due to its affinity for enzymes and simple synthesis, organic polymers, including synthetic polymers and biopolymers, are often used to modify magnetic nanoparticles. The abundant functional groups on the polymer surface can provide the required binding site for the enzyme. Xie et al. [41] engineered a magnetic composite poly(styrene-methacrylic acid) microsphere by micro-emulsion copolymerization of styrene and methacrylic acid. The lipase was covalently attached to the carboxyl group on the surface of the magnetic composite microsphere through EDC. Notably, the conversion rate of biodiesel from olive oil reached $86\%$ using the immobilized biocatalytic system.
In biopolymers, chitosan and polydopamine are commonly used for material modification. Wan et al. [42] introduced a mesoporous polydopamine layer on the outer surface of the hollow magnetic nanoparticles by a rapid interface-directed co-assembly strategy. Compared with the traditional $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles, the hollow magnetic nanoparticles exhibited high magnetization, low density, large specific surface area, and good monodispersity. Based on this strategy, the shell structure and pore size (11.53−49.53 nm) can be controlled by adjusting the ratio of the two templates in the emulsion system. Lipase was immobilized on the dopamine-coated magnetic nanoparticles through physical adsorption, and the effects of pore structure on catalytic activity and lipase loading were investigated. Immobilized lipase was applied to produce biodiesel by esterification of oleic acid.
Chitosan exhibits excellent properties such as non-toxicity, mucus-adhesion, mild synthesis conditions, good biocompatibility, and biodegradability [43]. Generally,
there are two kinds of magnetic chitosan synthesis strategies including one-step synthesis and two-steps synthesis. For one-step synthesis, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ magnetic nanoparticles are prepared in situ in chitosan [44, 45], whereas for two-steps synthesis, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ magnetic nanoparticles are first synthesized, and then modified by chitosan [46, 47]. Immobilization of the enzyme on magnetic chitosan is usually achieved by covalent attachment [48]. Xie et al. [49] immobilized Candida rugosa lipase on magnetic chitosan nanoparticles by GA. The resultant magnetic chitosan nanoparticles immobilized lipase was found to be monodisperse with an average diameter of $21\mathrm{nm}$ . The conversion ratio of biodiesel from soybean oil using the immobilized lipase reached $87\%$ . It also showed remarkable recycling potential retaining $72\%$ of its preliminary conversion efficiency after 4 repeated cycles.   
Graphene oxide has various functional groups such as carboxyl groups, hydroxyl groups, and epoxides on its surface, which makes it suitable for modification of magnetic nanoparticles [50]. Therefore, magnetic nanoparticles modified by graphene oxide were also used as carriers for lipase immobilization [51,52]. Xie et al. [53] fabricated a graphene oxide nanocomposite by encapsulating $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ in graphene oxide. The amino group on the lipase (Candida rugosa lipase) was covalently linked to the carboxyl group on the graphene oxide using EDC and NHS. The yield of biodiesel produced by soybean oil using this magnetic biocatalyst was $92.8\%$ . The immobilized lipase could be recovered by an external magnetic field without significant loss of activity and reused for five cycles.   
Gold nanoparticles have also been used to develop magnetic nano biocatalysts for
improving the performance of catalytic biodiesel production [54]. nano-sized gold particles were grown directly on the surface of larger magnetic nanoparticles to form a snowman-like $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{Au}$ in the presence of oleic acid. The residual oleic acid chains on the surface of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{Au}$ increased the affinity for lipase, thus leading to interfacial activation [55,56]. TLL was immobilized on $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{Au}$ magnetic nanoparticles, and the resulting immobilized lipase was used to catalyze the production of biodiesel from tomato seed oil. Results demonstrated that the conversion rate of biodiesel reached up to $97.4\%$ using $10\%$ of the immobilized lipase (w/w of oil). This superior bio-catalytic performance was ascribed to the interfacial activation of lipase. The surface polarity of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{Au}$ nanoparticles induced the lipase to have a better orientation. Furthermore, Au was used as conduction centers to promote electron conduction.
Cross-linking enzyme aggregates (CLEAs) technology is an inexpensive, highly efficient, and support-free immobilization method. The preparation of CLEA comprises precipitation or aggregation of enzyme followed by chemical cross-linking of the resulting enzyme aggregates [57]. However, the size of CLEAs clusters increases when CLEAs are separated from the reaction mixtures by centrifugation or filtration, resulting in high mass transfer limitations. Furthermore, low mechanical stability of CLEAs may limit their industrial applications. In order to overcome these problems, CLEAs were immobilized on magnetic nanoparticles to obtain magnetic CLEAs (Fig. 2). The magnetic CLEAs (mCLEAs) could be easily recovered with magnets, thus avoiding irreversible aggregation of CLEAs caused by filtration or centrifugation. Furthermore, mCLEAs exhibited better thermal and storage stability [58]. Badoei-dalfard and co-workers [59] covalently linked lipase-CLEAs to APTES-functionalized magnetic nanoparticles by GA. In contrast to free lipase, the biodiesel production from waste cooking oil was $20\%$ increased by lipase-mCLEAs. Furthermore, lipase-mCLEAs exhibited high reusability, and could be reused up to 5 cycles with no significant decrease in catalytic activity. Cruz-Izquierdo et al. [60] used CALB-mCLEAs to produce biodiesel from waste frying oils and non-edible vegetable oils. The CALB-mCLEAs could be used continuously for at least 10 cycles. Mehde et al. [61] immobilized CALB-CLEAs and the free CALB on tannic acid-modified magnetic nanoparticles. After $2\mathrm{h}$ , the conversion rate of biodiesel by the immobilized lipase and lipase-mCLEAs reached $68\%$ and $86\%$ , respectively. Badoei-dalfard et al. [62] combined graphene oxide, CLEAs and magnetic nanoparticle technology to formulate magnetic graphene oxide nanocomposite biocatalytic system. Lipase-CLEAs were immobilized on APTES modified magnetic graphene oxide in the presence of GA. Biodiesel yield from citronella oil using lipase-mCLEAs was 3-folds higher than that to the free lipase. In conclusion, all these examples indicated that lipase-mCLEAs were an efficient biocatalyst for biodiesel production.
## 2.2 Lipase immobilization on/in silica nanoparticles
Silica nanoparticles have been widely used as enzyme immobilization carriers due to their low cost, non-toxicity, good biocompatibility, easy modification, large specific surface area, and controllable pore size [63-65]. Owing to the presence of silanol groups on the surface of the silica gel, it is convenient to modify the silica gel by silane with a functional group. 3-Glycidyloxypropyl trimethoxysilane (3-GPTMS) is a silane with an epoxy group at the end of the chain. The epoxy group has high reactivity and can be directly linked by reacting with groups on the enzyme molecule such as $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ and -HS. SBA-15 was modified by 3-GPTMS and used as carrier to immobilized Rhizomucor miehei lipase (RML), TLL, and CALB (Fig. 3) [66]. The loading efficiency of all three lipases on the modified SBA-15 was four times higher than the modified nano mesoporous silicon. The conversion rate of biodiesel by CALB-SBA, TLL-SBA, and RML-SBA was recorded to be $58.8\%$ , $98.6\%$ , and $95.4\%$ , respectively. Furthermore, the CALB-SBA, TLL-SBA, RML-SBA presented excellent reusability in several successive cycles.
Jun et al. [67] synthesized mesostructured onion-like silica (Meso-Onion-S) with large pores by changing the surfactant packing parameter of the solution. Lipase from Rhizopus oryzae was adsorbed into Meso-Onion-S and then cross-linked with glutaraldehyde. The immobilized lipase was used to produce biodiesel by transesterification of soybean oil with methanol in isooctane. Evidently, the catalytic performance of the immobilized lipase was 23-times higher than that of the free counterpart.
As a silica gel material with high surface area and open pores, aerogel has been exploited as a carrier for enzyme immobilization. Arumugam et al. [68] immobilized lipases on spherical nano aerogels to produce biodiesel from Hevea brasiliensis oil by simple physical adsorption. The immobilized biocatalyst showed a high conversion rate of biodiesel $(93\%)$ and was able to preserve $90\%$ of its initial activity after 10
consecutive cycles.
In addition, silicon-based organic-inorganic hybrid compounds have also been used for enzyme immobilization. Macario et al. [69] encapsulated lipase in organic-inorganic hybrid nanospheres with a porous silica shell for biodiesel production. In this hybrid biocatalyst, the lipid bilayer membrane of the liposome can provide a micro water system for the enzyme to maintain its conformation, while the porous inorganic silica shell can stabilize the liposome phase protecting the lipase molecules. The resultant immobilized lipase displayed similar activity to that of the free lipase and could be recycled five times.
## 2.3 Lipase immobilization on/in metal-organic frameworks nanoparticles
Metal-organic frameworks are popular porous materials that have been studied in recent decades. MOFs have a three-dimensional network composed of metal nodes and connected organic ligand bridges. Due to the presence of abundant metal nodes and ligands, the chemical function, crystal morphology, pore shape and size of MOFs can be controlled [70]. High surface area and the ease of pore size adjustment make MOFs as interesting candidates for wider application in gas adsorption, catalysis, separation, and drug delivery [71]. Recently, MOFs have been used as excellent carriers for the enzyme immobilization. The MOF-enzymes biocomposites exhibited excellent biocatalytic properties, improved stability, and reusability [72]. Zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8), an MOF formed by coordination between $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions and 2-methylimidazole (HmIm), is a commonly used nanomaterial for enzyme encapsulation due to its extremely mild preparation conditions (Fig. 4). Lyu et
al. [73] first used ZIF-8 to embed PVP-dispersed lipase and achieved good enzyme activity recovery ( $88\%$ relative to a free enzyme). Adnan et al. [74] encapsulated RML in X-shaped ZIF-8 to construct a novel biocatalyst for the production of biodiesel from soybean oil in an isooctane system. Under the optimized operating conditions, the conversion rate reached $95.6\%$ . Furthermore, the lipase $@$ ZIF-8 composites still retained $84.7\%$ of the initial activity after repeatedly use for 10 times. In addition to the ZIF-8, a wide range of other MOF materials have also been envisioned to the encapsulation of lipase. Rafiei et al. [75] introduced a new method of encapsulating Candida rugosa lipase (CRL) in ZIF-67, a MOF consists of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and Hmim (Fig. 5). The newly developed CRL@ZIF-67 biocatalytic system maintained good catalytic potential in 6 repeated cycles for biodiesel production by solvent-free transesterification of soybean oil.   
Due to the highly porous structures of some MOFs, lipases can also be physically adsorbed into the cages of mesoporous MOFs instead of encapsulation into MOFs. For example, Adnan et al. [76] synthesized ZIF-8 using CTAB (cetyltrimethylammonium bromide) as a morphogen regulator and histidine (His) as a cofactor. The synthesized hierarchical ZIF-8 has a pore size of $5.20\mathrm{nm}$ . Subsequently, Burkholderia cepacia lipase (BCL) was immobilized onto this mesoporous ZIF-8 by adsorption and used to produce biodiesel. The conversion rate of biodiesel by BCL@ZIF-8 reached $93.4\%$ with no significant decrease in the activity of BCL@ZIF-8 after 8 cycles.
In addition to pore adsorption, lipases were also immobilized on the surface of MOF by hydrophobic interactions [77]. Hu et al. [78] obtained polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) coated hydrophobic UiO-66 by chemical vapor deposition (CVD), and used as a carrier to immobilize Aspergillus niger lipase (ANL) by simple physical adsorption (ANL $@$ UiO-66-PDMS). The ANL $@$ UiO-66-PDMS showed superior catalytic performance for biodiesel production by methanolysis of soybean oil. It also exhibited higher catalytic activity, activity recovery, methanolysis performance and methanol tolerance than ANL $@$ UiO-66 counterpart. Importantly, the ANL $@$ UiO-66-PDMS demonstrated a noteworthy reusability and retained $91.6\%$ of activity after ten consecutive re-use operations.
Novel immobilized enzyme carriers by combining MOF with other materials have attracted wide attention in the recent years [79, 80]. Liu et al. [81] converted MIL-100 (Al) into nanopores carbon materials (NPCs) by thermal conversion (cMOF), and used as a carrier for enzymes immobilization BCL was immobilized on this cMOF by adsorption and applied to produce biodiesel from soybean oil and ethanol. It was found that the carbonization temperature can control the pore size and carboxyl amount of cMOF, thereby affecting the enzyme loading and catalytic performance. This nanostructured material combines the morphological structure of MOF with the high stability of carbon, thus indicating a potential utility for enzyme immobilization and biodiesel production.
## 2.4 Lipase immobilization on/in carbon nanotubes
Because of their strong mechanical properties and chemical stability, carbon nanotubes are considered to be very effective support for enzyme immobilization.
Acid treatment to carboxylate the surface of carbon nanotubes is the most commonly used method for the functionalization of carbon nanotube [82]. Bencez et al. [83] used different lengths of the linker to immobilize CALB on carboxylated single-walled carbon nanotubes (SWCNTs) by covalent attachment. It was found that the length of the linker had a significant effect on the activity of the immobilized enzyme, and the shorter the linker, the higher the enzyme activity. In the production of biodiesel from sunflower oil and ethanol, the optimized conversion rate of $93.1\%$ was achieved by the immobilized lipase. The immobilized lipase retained $90\%$ of initial activity. However, the high price of SWCNTs limits its broad spectrum applications. In contrary, multi-walled carbon nanotubes (MWCNTs) are regarded as more economical [84]. Deep et al. [85] covalently linked lipase onto carboxylated MWCNTs via EDC and NHS as cross-linkers for biodiesel production from jatropha oil. The MWCNTs-insolubilized biocatalyst showed $90\%$ conversion rate of biodiesel within 1 h of the reaction. However, the carbon nanotubes are small, which makes the recovery of the carbon nanotubes immobilized enzyme after the reaction a difficult task. Wang et al. [86] formulated a Pickering emulsion based on MWCNTs to address this problem. For this, CALB was covalently attached to amino-functionalized MWCNTs by using glutaraldehyde. The CALB-loaded MWCNTs were then added to $n$ -heptane with a small amount of PBS, and the MWCNTs-stabilized water-in-oil Pickering emulsion was obtained by ultra-sonication. The obtained CALB $@$ PE was a micron-sized water-encapsulated capsule with CALB $@$ MWCHTs as the outer shell. The difference in density between the aqueous capsule and the organic phase allowed
CALB $@$ PE to sink quickly in the organic phase, making it easy to recycle. The prepared CALB $@$ PE showed high efficacy for biodiesel production from J. curcas L. seed oil with a corresponding conversion rate of $95.2\%$ . This high conversion rates may be caused by activation of the lipase at the oil-water interface [87,88].
Apart from their individual applicability, MWCNTs can also be combined with magnetic nanoparticles for the immobilization of multiple enzymes [89]. Although magnetism can solve the problem of recycling, the active sites of carbon nanotubes are not sufficient and can only be introduced by the functionalization of carbon nanotubes with dendrimer molecules [90]. Fan and coworkers immobilized a variety of lipases onto this dendrimer-coated magnetic multi-walled carbon nanotubes (m-MWCNTs-PAMAM) and successfully applied to biodiesel production [91-93].
## 2.5 Lipase immobilization on/in organic polymers
Organics, especially polymers, have always been important candidates for enzyme immobilization support. For example, the supports of commercial immobilized lipase Novozymes 435 is a polymer synthesized by cross-linking divinylbenzene and methacrylic acid [21]. Compared with inorganic inert materials, polymers show better protein affinity, which can reduce the possible negative effects of the carrier on the enzyme [94,95]. The main advantage of polymers is that the monomers and the functional groups carried by the monomers can be selected according to the enzymes immobilized. These functional groups can facilitate the binding of the enzyme to the support or increase the affinity of the substrate and the immobilized enzyme, thereby increasing the enzyme activity. Functional groups such as hydroxyl, carboxyl, amino, and epoxy groups can provide sites for covalent binding of enzymes, and various alkyl groups can provide hydrophobicity. These characteristics make it widely used in the modification of inorganic nanomaterials such as magnetic nanoparticles, which have been introduced previously. Dumri et al. [96] encapsulated silver nanoparticles with polydopamine using self-polymerization of dopamine under the alkaline conditions. PPL adhered on dopamine was then used to produce biodiesel from soybean oil.
In lipase immobilization, the applied pure polymer materials are mainly large-sized materials such as foams, membranes, and particles above the micron level. The polymer nanomaterials used in lipase immobilization include nanoparticles and nanofibers. Although nanoparticles such as polystyrene (PS) [97], poly(methyl methacrylate) (PMMA) [98], poly(urea-urethane) (PUU) [99-101], poly-DL-lactic acid (PDLLA) [95] nanoparticles immobilized lipase have been reported, but there is no in-depth study of whether they are suitable for biodiesel production. Liu et al. [102] reported that polyacrylamide nanogel encapsulated lipase exhibited high efficiency for biodiesel production by catalytic reaction of soybean oil and methanol in a two-phase system containing $10\%$ water. The amphiphilic polymer shell of the nanogel facilitated the immobilized lipase to be positioned at the oil-water interface. At the same time, the presence of the polymer outer shell also improved the stability of the lipase under high temperature and methanol concentration. The structure of the nanomaterial has a great impact on the catalytic performance of the immobilized enzyme. Nanofibers not only have pore structure with less diffusion resistance, but also large specific surface area and promising enzyme loading capacity, which are requisite characteristics for enzyme immobilization [103].The unique shape and structure of the nanofiber makes it easy to recover from the reaction system and to implicate in continuous operations while having the advantages of nanomaterials [104]. In recent years, the development of electrospinning technology has made great progress in the immobilization of enzymes on electrospun nanofibers [105]. Sakai et al. [106] immobilized Pseudomonas cepacia lipase on electrospun poly(acrylonitrile) (PAN) nanofibrous by simple physical adsorption method for biodiesel production from rapeseed oil. A high conversion rate of $94\%$ ascertained the feasibility of PAN nanofibrous immobilized enzyme in biodiesel production. By applying different immobilization strategies, Li et al. [107] activated the nitrile group (CN functional group) of the PAN nanofiber membrane by amidination reaction, and then covalently coupled with the Pseudomonas cepacia lipase (Fig. 6). The resulting immobilized biocatalyst was capable of producing soybean oil-derived biodiesel with exceptional thermal, storage and reusability properties.
Liu et al. [108] used a distinctive strategy to immobilize lipase onto electrospun nanofibers, and found that the presence of solid salts had a significant effect on the lipase activity in the non-aqueous phase catalysis [109,110]. LiCl is a salt that is slightly soluble in organic solvents and has been widely used as an electrolyte additive in the fabrication of electrospun nanofibers [111]. The same group found that LiCl embedded in polyurethane fibers could irreversibly adsorb proteins [112]. They co-electrospun LiCl with polyurethane nanofibers and adsorbed bovine serum albumin (BSA) as the first layer, and then cross-linked lipase AK with outer layer BSA by GA. It was found that the LiCl content in nanofibers had a significant effect on the catalytic activity and stability of the immobilized enzymes. In the application of catalytic production of biodiesel in isooctane, the LiCl containing nanofibers immobilized lipase presented 6.6 times higher activity than LiCl free counterpart. The conversion rate of biodiesel by LiCl-containing nanofiber biocatalyst reached $91\%$ within $2\mathrm{h}$ . Furthermore, the biocatalyst could be repeatedly used for 42 times without a significant loss of activity, whereas, LiCl-free nanofibers lost more than $90\%$ of the enzyme activity after 13 cycles
## 2.6 Lipase immobilization on/in other nano hybrid materials
As a new type of enzyme immobilization support, the hybrid nanoflowers (Fig. 7) comprising of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3(}\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{2}$ and proteins have received extensive attention since first reported [113]. Jiang et al. [114] applied lipase inorganic hybrid nanoflowers to the preparation of biodiesel from sunflower oil. The conversion rate of biodiesel using lipase hybrid nanoflowers reached $96.5\%$ with retention of $72.5\%$ activity after 5 successive cycles. Experimental results indicated that the hybrid nanoflower were an economically viable and highly promising biodiesel catalyst due to simple preparation methods and good yields.
## 2.7 Advantages and disadvantages of the nano-immobilized lipases
Immobilization of the enzyme on support materials is mainly intended to overcome the deficiencies of the free enzyme such as poor operation stability, difficult recovery and lack of recyclability. Enzyme immobilization technology also allows continuous reactors to be used in enzymatic catalytic production. However, there are still some problems with the traditional supports such as non-uniform, non-porous, and long-range ordering from the atomic to microscale. Furthermore, shortcomings such as low enzyme loading, poor dispersion, and mechanical stability also restrict the further application of traditional support materials for enzyme immobilization.
In recent years, nanomaterials have received extensive attention as enzyme immobilization supports. The advantages and disadvantages of the nano-immobilized lipases are summarized in Table 2. Both porous and non-porous nanomaterials have large surface areas due to their small size. The activity of the immobilized enzyme is related to how crowded the enzyme is on the support [31]. The large surface area of the nanomaterials allows the maximal enzyme loading and substantially improved catalytic activity per unit mass. Generally, the catalytic reaction of biodiesel is carried out in the organic phase, where the free lipase will agglomerate, leading to an increase in mass transfer limitation. By contrast, the lipase immobilized on the surface of the non-porous nanomaterial avoids the aggregation of the enzyme, thus allowing high enzyme loading with very little mass transfer limitation. The mass transfer limitations in the porous nanomaterials are also lower due to more pore volume „near the surface‟ than the conventional porous materials. In addition, nanomaterials have good mechanical and chemical stability, and better dispersibility. The particle size can be adjusted as desired [115].
However, nanomaterials also exhibit also notable disadvantage. The small particle size of the nanomaterial resulted in difficult separation from the substrate, and thus increasing the downstream costs (usually requiring ultracentrifugation or nanofiltration). In addition, the small size limits its use in continuous reactors. Application of magnetic nanoparticles is the most commonly used method to solve this issue. Superparamagnetic nanoparticles are easier to separate while preserving the advantages of nanomaterials. Moreover, magnetic nanoparticles can be used in fluidized bed continuous reactors [116]. However, the preparation process of magnetic nanoparticles is usually complicated and costly. Furthermore, the magnetic properties of nanoparticles are easily lost during their modification procedures. Therefore, it is necessary to develop new nanomaterials with excellent properties for enzyme immobilization [106].
## 3.  Influencing factors on biodiesel production using the immobilized lipase
## 3.1 Lipase source
Lipases, also known as triacylglycerol hydrolases, are mainly used to catalyze the hydrolysis of long-chain insoluble glycerides in aqueous media. They have been classified as serine hydrolase, and the active center is a catalytic triad composed of a nucleophilic serine, an aspartic acid or glutamic acid, and a histidine [117, 118]. As a widely used multifunctional enzyme, lipase can also catalyze transesterification, ester synthesis and aminolysis with high enantiomeric and regioselectivity [119]. Its high versatility makes it a widely used biocatalyst in food, washing, pharmaceutical, fuel, leather, textile, cosmetics, and paper and other industries [120]. Most of the enzymes catalyze water-soluble substrates. However, the natural substrate triglycerides catalyzed by lipase are very poorly soluble in aqueous solution that makes lipase an interfacial enzyme [121]. Most of the lipases have a polypeptide chain with a hydrophilic and a hydrophobic side. In a homogeneous saline medium, the hydrophobic region of the lid interacts with the hydrophobic areas surrounding the active center to maintain a closed-form of lipase. When the substrate droplets are present, an interface is formed with the water. Under the interaction of the hydrophobic interface, the lipases open the lid and are attached to the interface through its larger hydrophobic pocket. In this way, the lipase can attack the insoluble substrate directly. The conformation of the lipase in this state is activated while the lipase activity increases sharply, which is called interface activation [77].
Even though the active centers are similar, lipases from different sources exhibit different selectivity to their substrates. They catalyze the reaction of a wide variety of substrates, but the rate of reaction varies greatly depending on the molecular structure of the substrate and the type of lipase. The size and hydrophobicity of the catalytic pocket of lipase directly affects its specificity. The amphiphilic structure located near the active center of lipase, which is commonly known as the “lid”, also has a significant effect on the catalytic activity of lipase [122,123]. The production of biodiesel mainly uses lipases transesterification ability to convert long-chain triglycerides into FAAEs. At the same time, lipases can also catalyze the ester synthesis reaction of FFAs with alcohol to utilize the feedstocks with high FFAs content. However, lipases used in biodiesel production should be non-stereospecific so that all tri-, di, monoglycerides and FFAs of different lengths could be converted to FAAE [124]. The use of biocatalysts composed of a mixture of different lipases is an effective strategy in the catalytic reaction of composite substrates such as biodiesel production [125]. Transesterification of triglycerides with alcohol has multiple steps of the reaction. Although non-specific lipases such as CALB can be used, the reaction rate in a particular step is still limited. Shahedi et al. [126] co-immobilized 1,3-specific RML and non-specific CALB on epoxy functionalized silica gel. The 1,3-specific lipase RML accelerated the rate-determining acyl migration step in biodiesel production, increasing the catalytic activity of biocatalysts and the conversion of biodiesel [127,128]. In addition, the structure of lipase can also be changed by covalent and hydrophobic interactions during the immobilization to reduce its specificity and improve the biodiesel catalytic ability [62,77]. Lipases are produced by various organisms, animal, plants and microbial strains. Lipases from different origins have different physicochemical properties and catalytic performance, which leads to differences in the corresponding immobilization methods and production processes. Therefore, lipases for biodiesel production need to be inexpensive and stable for long-term supply. Microbial lipases obtained from fungi, yeast, and bacteria are a better choice to fulfil this requirement. The lipase-producing microorganisms should have high specificity and diversity, amenable to genetic modification and can be easily applied to industrial production of lipase [129]. Generally, lipases used in biodiesel production are derived from microorganisms, such as Aspergillus niger [78], Burkholderia cepacian [81], Candida Antarctica [83, 86], Candida rugose [75], Pseudomonas cepacian [107], Pseudomonas fluorescens [130], Rhizopus oryzae [67], Rhizomucor miehei [74] and Thermomyces lanuginosus [36]. In addition, animal-derived porcine pancreatic lipase can now be produced recombinantly by transgenic bacteria with a significant increase in yield [131]. Therefore, PPL, lipase of animal origin, has also received much attention in the study of biocatalytic biodiesel [96].
## 3.2 Immobilization methods
Generally, methods of enzymes immobilization include adsorption, covalent attachment, encapsulation, and cross-linking (Fig. 8). Adsorption is the simplest and most common method of immobilization. The enzyme is immobilized onto the carrier support by weak interactions such as “van der Waals” forces, hydrogen bonding and hydrophobic interactions. Due to the weak interactions, the adsorption method usually displays obvious enzyme leakage problems. Adsorption processes are commonly applied to porous supports such as mesoporous silicon, or mesoporous MOFs due to their large specific surface area [68,132]. The presence of porous structures can efficiently trap enzymes and minimize enzyme leakage, as well as protect the enzyme molecules from external denaturant conditions to some extent. In lipase immobilization and biodiesel production, the hydrophobicity of the support has a special effect on the enzyme activity. As mentioned earlier, lipases have a natural affinity for the hydrophobic interface that allows their immobilization on the hydrophobic support to be different from the conventional enzyme hydrophobic adsorption [77]. Studies have shown that lipase immobilization is rapid and strong by hydrophobic adsorption [133-135]. Due to the activation of the interface, the lipase is effectively immobilized on the hydrophobic support in a monomeric open form. This allows the lipases no longer to be opened by other external interfaces. Furthermore, the open state of the lipases can be stably maintained in the hydrophobic support [133]. In addition, hydrophobic support has a high affinity to hydrophobic triacylglycerols and fatty acid esters but exhibits no affinity to glycerol. These properties contribute to the contact of the enzyme with the substrate and the product away from the catalytic site in biodiesel production [86]. Therefore, the hydrophobicity of the support has a significant impact on the production of biodiesel [78,86].
In covalent immobilization, the enzyme is covalently bound to the support by functional groups present on the surface. Covalent immobilization is a strong chemical bond that ensures high adhesion of the lipases to the supports with no or negligible leakage. The covalent attachment usually does not occur spontaneously and requires additional processing of the enzymes or supports to obtain a more active functional group. Bifunctional reagents such as glutaraldehyde are commonly used for the covalent immobilization of enzymes. Glutaraldehyde can be spontaneously covalently linked to the amino group on the support and enzyme under the mild conditions [136]. This property makes it often useful for the efficient immobilization of enzymes on supports with or without modification with amino groups [37,62,137]. Glutaraldehyde can also increase the hydrophobicity of the supports [119]. The dehydration condensation reagents such as EDC and DIC (diisopropyl carbodiimide) are also commonly used for the immobilization of enzymes. EDC is generally used in conjunction with NHS to help form an amide bond between the amino group and the carboxyl group. This allows it to conjugate with the amino groups on the enzyme to the carboxyl groups of the support or vice versa [38,53]. In addition, the covalent immobilization can be achieved directly by mixing with the enzymes by the modification of some carriers with active functional groups such as epoxy functional groups [66,126].
In some supports, multi-point covalent binding can also provide additional stability to the immobilized lipase [119]. When lipase is attached to the support by multiple covalent chains, the structure of the enzyme is fixed and rigid. This rigidity can maintain the native structure of the lipase against denaturing conditions such as heat, organic solvents, and extreme pH. Multi-point immobilization also maintains the open conformation of lipase. However, this method needs to preserve the active conformation of lipase, otherwise it will lead to inactivation of lipase [138]. Encapsulation immobilization typically incorporates the enzyme in porous support by a co-precipitation method. This method rarely has the problem of leakage of enzymes. Encapsulation has good compatibility with the enzymes, and this method is usually simpler and faster due to the simultaneous completion of support synthesis and enzyme immobilization [74]. Therefore, this method has high requirements for support [139]. However, high mass transfer resistance is an unavoidable problem in the encapsulation immobilization.
The enzymes are interconnected by crosslinking agents by using a most commonly used cross-linker glutaraldehyde. The main advantage of cross-linking is the formation of multiple covalent linkages within or between enzyme molecules.
Multi-point covalent attachment enhances the rigidity of the lipase. This increases the resistance of the immobilized enzyme to heat and organic solvents, which is useful for the industrial production of biodiesel in the presence of alcohols such as methanol [61].
Generally, the simultaneous use of cross-linking and other immobilization methods can significantly improve the catalytic performance of enzymes. For example, Jun et al. incorporated an additional step of cross-linking after the adsorption had been completed [67]. Furthermore, the formation of cross-linked enzyme occurred in the pores of the supports, which can effectively prevent the leaching of the enzyme. Besides, many combinations of covalent linkages and cross-linking have been envisioned to increase the enzyme loading and stability of the immobilized enzyme [59-61].
## 3.3 Feedstocks
Biodiesel can be obtained from a variety of raw materials such as vegetable oils, animal fats, microbial oils, and algae oils [140]. The price of raw materials, the size of the plant, and the value of by-product glycerin are the main influencing factors of biodiesel production cost, among which the cost of the raw material is the most critical variable [141]. According to the source and availability of the feedstock, it can be divided into edible, non-edible and waste oil [142]. The production of biodiesel feedstocks from edible vegetable oils such as soybean oil and palm oil is stable and economically viable [11]. However, the use of edible oil as a raw material competes with the food market. This leads to the economic feasibility of this production method
that needs to be reconsidered [143].
In recent years, researchers have rekindled their attention to non-edible oils. The use of non-edible oils can reduce the price of raw materials and avoid competition with the food market [144]. For example, the oil content of Jatropha seeds reaches $30\%$ to $50\%$ of its weight [145]. Jatropha can grow on wasteland with little demand for water and fertilizer. The presence of toxic compounds such as phorbol esters limits its food applications but has no adverse effect on biodiesel production [146]. Unfortunately, the high FFA and water content caused the undesired alkaline-catalyzed transesterification reaction process for some non-edible oils such as jatropha oil, rubber seed oil, etc.
Whether edible oil or non-edible oil, it is based on agricultural production. This requires a large amount of land, water, fertilizer, and other resources to produce raw materials, which still compete with food production [147]. This will also cause new problems such as deforestation that exert tangible impacts on environmental protection and sustainable development. In this context, the use of microalgae oil that does not occupy land is an attractive option. Microalgae oil is currently considered as one of the most promising alternative sources of non-edible oils for biodiesel [144, 148]. However, the main limitation is the high cost of obtaining algae oil, including high cost of algae farming, oil recovery and separation [140]. Therefore, large-scale biodiesel production from algal oil requires further research.
Waste oils include waste cooking oil, frying oil and other waste byproduct such as tall oil (a byproduct of the pulp and paper industry), waste animal fat (major wastes of the meat industry, the leather industry and fish processing), and tobacco seed oil (byproduct of tobacco leaf production) [10,146]. The daily cooking and food industries produce huge amounts of waste cooking oil and frying oil every day. Use of waste oil as a raw material is a more attractive option because of its obvious environmental benefits. Compared to edible and non-edible oils, the acquisition of waste oil does not require additional land or resources and is much cheaper. In addition, the use of waste oil can also help solve the problem of waste oil disposal and reduce the water pollution caused by it [149]. Waste animal fats contain more saturated fatty acids and free fatty acids. High temperatures promote the hydrolysis of triglycerides, which makes waste frying oils or cooking oils also usually contain high free fatty acids [19]. In addition, waste frying oil and cooking oil have high water content and contain other animal or vegetable oils depending on what is cooked [140]. However, the traditional alkali-based transesterification reaction generally requires low free fatty acids and water content [150]. Free fatty acid reacts with the basic catalyst to form soap, which makes the catalyst ineffective leading to the separation of downstream glycerol difficult. High water content also promotes the production of soap and affects the yield of FAAEs [151], therefore, the traditional alkali-based transesterification reaction for the production of biodiesel from waste oils is not suitable. In contrast, lipase-catalyzed transesterification is not sensitive to the free fatty acid content and water content of the feedstock [152]. Due to the versatility of lipases, free fatty acids in the oil can also be synthesized into FAAEs. In addition, immobilized enzyme-catalyzed biodiesel production has the advantage of no or negligible by-products, easy separation of glycerol, mild reaction conditions, and catalyst recycling in multiple batches. These characteristic features render immobilized lipases a noteworthy biocatalyst for the production of biodiesel from waste oils and non-edible oils with high free fatty acid content.
## 3.4 Operation mode
An important advantage of biocatalytic production of biodiesel is the simple production process. Fig. 9 and Fig. 10 compare the operations of enzyme-catalyzed and typical alkali-catalyzed biodiesel production. It is noted that the downstream operation of the base catalysis method is more complicated. Glycerol needs to be recovered by additional steps such as evaporation of methanol and removal of saponification products. In addition, the treatment of alkaline wastewater is also a problem. The transesterification reaction is reversible, and usually an excess of methanol is added to the reaction to push the reaction forward [153]. However, due to the obvious damage caused by methanol to enzymes, a lower methanol ratio is usually used in enzyme-catalyzed biodiesel production and added stepwise. This makes the enzyme-catalyzed reaction require a longer reaction time, but also reduces the methanol residue in the product. In addition, the enzyme-catalyzed reaction does not generate saponification products and does not produce a large amount of wastewater. These make the downstream operation of the enzymatic method mainly the separation of the immobilized enzyme, and the biodiesel and glycerol produced are of high purity [154].
For biocatalytic production of biodiesel, scaling up is also an important challenge.
The production process can be roughly divided into the batch reaction and the continuous reaction. As shown in Fig. 11, reactors such as stirred tank reactors (STRs), packed-bed reactors (PBRs) and fluidized bed reactors (FBRs) were used with the immobilized lipase in the production processes [155]. Studies on reactor-scale biodiesel production with nanomaterial immobilized lipase are summarized in Table
3.
Batch reactions are most commonly used and are usually carried out in STRs. Most of the research on the enzymatic production of biodiesel was conducted in the laboratory with batch reactors because of the simplicity of operation. In addition, the batch reactions exhibit high dispersion of the substrate, simple equipment, and easy reaction control [146]. Pseudomonas cepacia lipase immobilized on magnetic nanoparticles has been applied in reactor scale production of biodiesel [156]. The conversion of soybean oil in the stirred tank reactor ( $100\%$ in first 3 cycles) was much higher than in the flask (more than $55\%$ in first two cycles). The significant improvement in the conversion rate should be attributed to the better dispersion and mass transfer provided by the agitation systems with impellers. For nanomaterials, the separation of immobilized enzymes in batch reactions requires costly ultracentrifugation or nanofiltration. And the separation process usually results in loss of enzyme activity. These makes the study of magnetic nanomaterials important in reducing the cost of recovery and the loss of enzyme activity.
Batch processing usually has a long reaction time, so a large-capacity reactor is required for large-scale production. After the completion of a batch reaction, the reactor needs to be unloaded, cleaned and reloaded. All these steps increase the overall production cost of the batch reaction [1]. Another factor to be considered is that the high shear force generated by stirring leads to the inactivation of immobilized enzyme [157]. In industrial-scale production, continuous flow reactors have higher efficiency than the batch reaction [158]. The continuous process can be operated for a long time under stable conditions, avoiding the loss of productivity and enzyme activity caused by the intermediate process.
Among many types of continuous flow reactors, PBRs are the most commonly used for the lipase-catalyzed reactions. In a typical reaction using PBRs, the immobilized lipase is loaded into the column, and the substrate is continuously pumped into the column. It is worth noting that the size of immobilized enzyme should not be too small to keep the pressure drop within a reasonable range [159]. This fact makes larges size nanofibers more practicable for PBRs [106]. Sakai et al. [106] applied electrospun polyacrylonitrile nanofibers immobilized Pseudomonas cepacia lipase to the continuous production of butyl-biodiesel in a packed-bed reactor. During the first 20 days of continuous operation, there was no significant decrease in conversion. This result demonstrates the feasibility of using nanofiber biocatalyst to produce biodiesel in PBRs. In addition to nanofibers, the use of other materials to 'immobilize' nano biocatalysts is also feasible. A PBR system was successfully established with magnetic nanoparticle-immobilized lipase catalyst and cotton support [160]. The presence of cotton increases the space between nanomaterials, making it possible for the application of nanoparticles in PBRs. In a four-packed bed reactor, the biodiesel conversion rate is maintained above $88\%$ , showing high catalytic efficiency and stability.
FBR is considered to be a hybrid of STR and PBR. In FBR, the feedstock is injected from the bottom and flows upwards at a rate that can boost up the catalyst. Because there is no need to consider the pressure drop, catalysts with smaller particles than PBRs can be used. However, there are still problems in the application of nanoparticles. Traditional FBRs on gravity to achieve catalyst settling. Since the nanoparticles are difficult to settle or tend to float, they are easily carried away by the flowing material even at very low flow rates. Application of a circulating fluidized bed bioreactor with nano silicon immobilized lipase was attempted [161]. Although the problem of catalyst loss was solved through material circulation, it could only be operated under batch conditions. Adding additional separation devices to the fluidized bed can also solve the application of nano-catalysts [162]. Narayanan and Pandey [163] reported the study of a continuous catalytic production of biodiesel in an improved reverse fluidized bed bioreactor using nano-silicon immobilized lipase. Under optimized conditions, the bioreactor can achieve a conversion rate of more than $80\%$ . By constructing a 5-6 column combination, the capacity of the reactor can reach 3600 \~ 4500 L/h. Although continuous production on FBRs has been achieved, the cost of nanofiltration remains. Using magnetism to hold magnetic nanoparticles in a FBR may be a feasible method, but it has not been reported in nano-biocatalyst biodiesel production [116,164].
The scale-up study of using biocatalyst to produce biodiesel is the key to its application in large-scale production. Especially for nano-biocatalysts, because of their small size, the reactor and process need to be optimized. For STRs, PBRs and FBRs, there are related studies using nano biocatalysts to produce biodiesel, which proves their feasibility. However, there are also problems such as high cost of nano-biocatalyst separation, deactivation of the enzyme, and few continuous reaction schemes. Batch processing is more compatible with nanomaterial immobilized enzymes, while continuous processing is more suitable for industrial production. The designing of more efficient continuous processes for nano catalysts are important ways to further reduce production costs and achieving large-scale production of biodiesel. More reactor-scale studies are still needed to further reduce overall costs before nano biocatalysts can be applied to industrial scale.
## 3.5 Other factors
Glycerol is another key factor leading to a decrease in enzyme activity in biodiesel production. Glycerol is the major by-product of the transesterification reaction with good economic value. However, it tends to accumulate on the surface of the catalyst during the reaction that might increase the tendency of the reaction to proceed in the reverse direction. In addition, glycerol forms a hydrophilic environment around the catalyst, creating it problematic for the hydrophobic substrate to contact the enzyme [165]. The inhibition of glycerol can be reduced or eliminated to some extent by using organic solvents and hydrophobic carriers [78,166]. The use of an organic solvent reduces the viscosity of the reaction mixture, increases the solubility of methanol, and avoids the formation of two phases, thereby improving mass transfer efficiency. Some reports have shown that the use of organic solvents can reduce the effects of methanol and glycerol on the enzyme activity [166,167]. Commonly used organic solvents are hexane, petroleum ether, n-heptane, and tert-butanol. Among them, tert-butanol forms sterically hindered tert-butyl group due to its three methyl chains and therefore does not participate in the transesterification reaction. Despite its advantages, the toxicity and flammability of organic solvents limit its application. In addition to environmental concern organic solvents also increase the difficulty and cost of downstream separation, which is not necessarily economical. Therefore, most of the studies on the immobilized enzyme-catalyzed biodiesel production prefer to use solvent-free systems. In addition, new solvents such as ionic liquids, and supercritical carbon dioxide have also been studied as alternatives to organic solvents in biocatalytic biodiesel production [168-170].
## 4. Conclusion
The cost of lipase is one of the major challenges for the enzymatic production of biodiesel. Immobilization technology enables the re-use of the lipase and also makes continuous production possible. Nanomaterials are an emerging type of materials used for the immobilization of a wide range of different enzymes. Their small particle size, large surface area, and high enzyme loading capacity make them a particularly versatile tool for the development of immobilized enzymes. The resultant nanostructured immobilized biocatalytic system is can be efficiently applied in lipase-catalyzed transesterification to produce biodiesel. Biodiesel production is a special catalytic reaction. The substrate is a composite, insoluble in water and high in viscosity. Coupled with the interesting interface characteristics of lipase, the biocatalyst used in this reaction requires additional target-oriented research. An array of nanoscale enzyme support materials including novel materials such as nanoflowers, carbon nanotubes, electrospun nanofibers, MOFs, etc. have recently been studied for biodiesel production. nano biocatalysts exhibit high catalytic efficiency in biodiesel production. Multi-point covalent linkages, as well as pore structures all, contribute to the stability of lipase in biodiesel production. In addition, both the effect of support on electron transport and the hydrophobicity of the support surface have a positive effect on the transesterification reaction. The difficulty of recycling caused by small particles is the main disadvantage of nano-support. Magnetic nanoparticles and nanofibers can address the problem of recycling to a certain extent. The price of raw materials is the main influencing factors of biodiesel production cost. Edible oils and certain non-edible oils require a lot of agricultural resources and cause environmental pollution, which is obviously not desirable. Low-cost various waste oils are the most suitable choices. In the process of producing renewable energy, the problem of waste oil treatment can be solved at the same time, with high environmental benefits. The problem of high water content and free fatty acids in waste oil makes enzymatic production of biodiesel more necessary. In the future, it is important to examine the overall cost of using nano-biocatalysts and common biocatalysts in biodiesel production. Because of its high efficiency and recyclability, Nano-biocatalysts reduce the cost of enzymes, but increase the cost of nano-materials and downstream separation. These are the main reasons that limit the further application of nano-biocatalysts in the production of industrial biodiesel. Low-cost, easily-recyclable nanomaterials should become an important direction for future research. Efficient reactors are also an important way to reduce costs, but most nano biocatalyst research is currently limited to very small reaction systems (less than 10ml). Scale-up research is still in its preliminary stage, and more reactor scale research is needed, especially for continuous reactors. In this way, enzymatic biodiesel can be extended to more refineries as an environmentally friendly renewable energy source.
